&lt;p&gt;Hey everyone! I just launched PDFLabs, a free web toolkit for PDFs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The biggest difference between this and sites like iLovePDF is that it has no backend server. Everything runs locally in the browser using JavaScript (pdf-lib &amp;amp; pdf.js). When you drop a file to merge, split, or compress it, the file never actually leaves your device.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It has 13 tools right now:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Merge / Split / Reorganize / Delete / Rotate PDFs&lt;br&gt;
PDF to Images / Images to PDF / Text to PDF / Extract Text&lt;br&gt;
Compress PDF / Add Watermark / Add Page Numbers / PDF Info&lt;br&gt;
Try it out here: &lt;a href="https://roaring-mermaid-ba6504.netlify.app" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://roaring-mermaid-ba6504.netlify.app&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'm a solo developer, so I'd love to hear your feedback on the UI/UX, mobile responsiveness, and how it performs on larger files. Brutal honesty is appreciated!&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The history of shapewear is, in many ways, the history of women's relationship with their own bodies. From the whalebone corsets of the 16th century to the latex waist trainers of the 2010s, women have used undergarments to shape their silhouettes for over 500 years. But the story is more complex than a simple narrative of oppression versus liberation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In the 1500s, corsets were not about achieving an impossibly small waist — they were about creating a cylindrical torso that complemented the structured gowns of the era. By the Victorian era, however, tight-lacing had become a symbol of status and discipline. Women endured rib deformities and organ displacement in pursuit of the "ideal" figure.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The 1920s brought liberation — and a new kind of constraint. Flappers abandoned corsets but embraced girdles that flattened the bust and hips to achieve the straight, boyish silhouette that defined the Jazz Age. Every era, it seems, has its "ideal," and every era has the undergarments to enforce it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The 1960s introduced Lycra, revolutionizing &lt;a href="https://www.brabic.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;shapewear&lt;/a&gt; with stretch and comfort. The 1990s brought Spanx, turning shapewear into a multi-billion dollar industry. And today, in 2026, brands like Brabic are writing the next chapter — one defined not by rigid ideals, but by choice, comfort, and inclusivity.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What's different about today's shapewear? For one, the conversation has shifted. Women are no longer wearing shapewear because society demands a specific silhouette. They are wearing it because it makes them feel confident, &lt;a href="https://www.brabic.com/collections/shapewear" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;comfortable&lt;/a&gt;, and empowered. The Brabic customer isn't trying to become someone else — she's enhancing the body she already has.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The modern shapewear movement is also defined by size inclusivity. &lt;a href="https://www.brabic.com/collections/shapewear" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Brabic's range&lt;/a&gt; extends to 42DD and beyond, acknowledging that beauty comes in all shapes and sizes. The "ideal" is no longer a single number — it's whatever makes you feel like the best version of yourself.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;History teaches us that the relationship between women and their undergarments is always evolving. And today, for the first time, the direction of that evolution is being guided by women themselves.&lt;/p&gt;

